                "dc.description.abstract" : [ {
                  "value" : "It has become increasingly understood that gender plays a key role in how individuals experience climate change, and due to the gender roles women inhabit, they are often placed directly in the path of climate impacts. In many communities, climate now plays a significant factor in decisions to migrate. As climate impacts intensify political, economic, and social vulnerabilities, women’s experiences of climate migration can be significantly different to their male counterparts. In Bangladesh and Fiji, internal migration due to climate impacts has become increasingly common and women must navigate strong patriarchal structures as they attempt to relocate. With global estimates of climate migrants ranging from 30 to 140 million by 2050, it is essential the interaction between gender and climate migration are interrogated to better understand the barriers and difficulties women as climate migrants face and what support may be needed in the future.\r\nThrough a qualitative meta-analysis, this research explores the gender and climate migration nexus to gain a deeper understanding of how gender influences migratory decision-making and the outcomes of climate migration. It engages in a critical comparison of climate migration studies and assesses how gender norms in the local context of the research and the researcher’s own background have shaped research design and findings. Adopting an analytical framework informed by De Haas’ Aspirations-Capabilities theory and Tschakert & Neef’s (im)mobility cube, this thesis can comment on how gender and other socio-demographic factors shape the experiences of women as climate migrants. It suggests that climate migration can both expand and decrease women’s agency as patriarchal structures and traditional gender responsibilities are reshaped by changing environments. This research has also identified data gaps regarding how socio-demographics such as age, religion, and poverty interlink with gender to shape climate migration outcomes, resulting in a mismatch between government support programmes and the needs of women as climate migrants. This thesis further recommends that researchers critically assess how socio-demographic factors influence who is excluded from participating in research. Published research must be open and reflective of where the gaps of understanding female climate migration experiences could exist so as to better inform policy and development practice.",
